{"name":"Nature Retreat","hex":"#7b8787","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nature","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The way things are, the totality of all things in the physical universe and their order, especially the physical world in contrast to spiritual realms and flora and fauna as distinct from human conventions, art, and technology.","The particular way someone or something is, especially\nThe essential or innate characteristics of a person or thing which will always tend to manifest, especially in contrast to specific contexts, reason, religious duty, upbringing, and personal pretense or effort."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To endow with natural qualities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nature"},{"term":"retreat","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of pulling back or withdrawing, as from something dangerous, or unpleasant.\n\nThe act of reversing direction and receding from a forward position.\n Withdrawal by a military force from a dangerous position or from enemy attack.","The act of reversing direction and receding from a forward position."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To withdraw from a position, go back.\n To withdraw military forces.","To withdraw military forces."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/retreat"}],"entries":[{"title":"Retreat (spiritual)","blocks":[{"text":"The meaning of a spiritual retreat can be different for different religious communities. Spiritual retreats are an integral part of many Buddhist, Christian and Sufi communities. There are many different types of spiritual retreats such as wellness retreats, mindfulness retreats, spa retreats, adventure retreats, detox retreats, yoga retreats, and religious retreats."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/000000000_WinShape.jpg/330px-000000000_WinShape.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Retreat_(spiritual)","wikidata":"Q19299086"},{"title":"Nature","blocks":[{"text":"Nature, in the broadest sense, is the physical world or universe. In this general sense nature refers to the laws, elements and phenomena of the physical world, including life. Although humans are part of nature, human activity or humans as a whole are often described as at times at odds, or outright separate and even superior to nature."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg/330px-Startrails_above_Gunung_Bromo_-_Indonesia.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nature","wikidata":"Q7860"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/BJ23-chapel.jpg/500px-BJ23-chapel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/BJ23-chapel.jpg/1280px-BJ23-chapel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/BJ23-chapel.jpg/500px-BJ23-chapel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An aspiring seminarian prays during a vocational discernment retreat in the chapel of Blessed John XXIII National Seminary in Massachusetts","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABJ23-chapel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Young_monk_in_meditation_cell%2C_Yerpa%2C_Tibet._1993.jpg/500px-Young_monk_in_meditation_cell%2C_Yerpa%2C_Tibet._1993.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Young_monk_in_meditation_cell%2C_Yerpa%2C_Tibet._1993.jpg/500px-Young_monk_in_meditation_cell%2C_Yerpa%2C_Tibet._1993.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Yo